WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:05.130
On what happens when two

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.633
directors depict the same event
using radically different approaches.

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:13.304
If you were one of the viewers
of the first nuclear detonation

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:18.059
on July 16th, 1945, the time between
when the blinding light of the explosion

00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:21.438
first hit your eyes and when you first
heard the sound of that explosion

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:26.735
would have been around 30 to 90 seconds,
depending on where you were located.

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:31.031
So when Christopher Nolan incorporates
this sound delay into Oppenheimer,

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:35.326
not only is it
accurate and dramatically effective,

00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:39.622
but it's also a great example of the use
of subjective perspective in a film.

00:00:40.331 --> 00:00:43.334
While the scene doesn't play out
from the perspective of a single viewer,

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:47.422
it is strictly limited to the perspective
of the observers themselves.

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:51.134
While he's showing the explosions
and makes that very calculated decision

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:55.597
to never give us a more distant,
objective overview of the scene

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:58.808
like he did here
just moments earlier in the film.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:02.729
Instead, he grounds us in the immediacy
of what the characters

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:03.730
we've been following throughout

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.733
the film would have actually experienced
on that day.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.401
Even when we do see the explosion.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.238
It's always from the angle that
the observers would have seen it from.

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:16.993
This scene is a great example
of sonic and visual subjectivity in film.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.873
Now compare that to this

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:24.834
the way David Lynch depicts

00:01:24.834 --> 00:01:28.379
the exact same event in Episode
eight of Twin Peaks The Return.

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:32.175
What we get here is essentially
a two minute unbroken take that flies us

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:35.428
directly into the heart
of the explosion, down to the level

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.889
where the atoms themselves are splitting
apart.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:38.681
It's a perspective

00:01:38.681 --> 00:01:40.475
that's completely detached from the way

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:42.936
any real person
could have experienced this event.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:46.606
And it's a great example
of objective perspective in film.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.234
I think this scene was one
of the most effective in Oppenheimer,

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:52.695
and this scene was one of my favorites
in the entire third season of Twin Peaks.

00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:55.782
And it's not
because I love watching things blow up.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:59.285
It's because of the way
each of these scenes uses perspective.

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:01.037
This video is sponsored by Nebula.

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:04.541
Get 40% off the annual plan and support
my work when you sign up today.

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:06.501
Lynch is treating the explosion

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:10.255
as a kind of abstract
metaphysical event in his story,

00:02:10.421 --> 00:02:14.592
and so depicting it in a way no actual
real life person could have experienced.

00:02:14.592 --> 00:02:17.595
It makes sense for the story he's telling.

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:21.808
Nolan, on the other hand, is telling
the story of Oppenheimer himself.

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:27.397
So focusing on a more individual subjective experience serves his story best.

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:30.316
I saw

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.528
Barbie the day after Oppenheimer,
and my favorite scene from that film

00:02:33.528 --> 00:02:37.448
also included a very strong use
of subjective perspective

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:41.202
For most of the film we're
following Barbie narratively in the story,

00:02:41.369 --> 00:02:44.539
but visually the camera is more clinical,
objective and removed,

00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:47.792
emphasizing
the studio artifice of Barbie's world

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:51.546
that Greta Gerwig
is intentionally drawing our attention to.

00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:55.967
But when Barbie leaves this artificial,
corporately constructed fantasy land

00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:59.971
and for the first time experiences
human emotions in the real world,

00:03:00.221 --> 00:03:03.224
look at what happens to the film's
visual perspective.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:07.437
In this moment,

00:03:07.437 --> 00:03:10.440
it's like we've suddenly slipped
into a different kind of movie

00:03:10.565 --> 00:03:14.402
and simultaneously into Barbie's
literal point of view.

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:17.280
I think it's a wonderful depiction
of these moments in life

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.659
where you kind of wake up to reality
fakeness of the corporately

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:26.372
constructed modern world just slips away
and you see life kind of as it really is.

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:30.793
People living, experiencing difficult,
complicated and wonderful emotions.

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:33.546
But the power of this moment doesn't
just come from

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:37.300
Greta Gerwig putting us literally
in Barbie's visual perspective.

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:41.596
She's also using the shift in the style
of filmmaking

00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:46.809
to reflect Barbie's
emotional internal state to the viewer.

00:03:46.851 --> 00:03:50.271
The film is taking on Barbie's
emotional perspective

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.776
because the filmmaking style
is reflecting Barbie's

00:03:54.776 --> 00:03:58.613
emotional experience,
how Gerwig chooses to shoot and construct

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:02.533
the scene doesn't reflect Ken's
experience, another Barbie's experience,

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:06.996
or just vaguely the experience of everyone
who's living in that moment

00:04:06.996 --> 00:04:08.122
in the world.

00:04:08.122 --> 00:04:11.209
It reflects Barbie's experience
specifically.

00:04:11.626 --> 00:04:13.169
So this is an example of how

00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:16.047
not just the position of the camera
and what we're seeing, but

00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:20.218
the filmmaking style itself
can reflect an emotional perspective.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.971
When we think about and talk
about perspective in film,

00:04:22.971 --> 00:04:27.809
most of what we think of is this When we
talk about objectivity and subjectivity,

00:04:27.809 --> 00:04:32.855
often we're talking about camera movements
or the position or angle of the camera.

00:04:32.855 --> 00:04:36.359
But perspective and film runs
much deeper than this, and I think it's

00:04:36.359 --> 00:04:40.613
actually one of the most powerful tools
cinema has at its disposal.

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:44.033
It can be the emotional heart
of an entire story.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:47.620
It can be shaped by everything
from performances to editing

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:49.998
and it starts
with how you write your story.

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:53.835
But it's also a complicated idea that
I don't think it's talked about enough.

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:56.713
So let's try to untangle some of this.

00:04:56.713 --> 00:04:58.881
What is perspective in its deepest sense?

00:04:58.881 --> 00:05:01.968
How is it formed
and how does it impact a film?

00:05:02.218 --> 00:05:05.596
Because whether you're a filmmaker
looking to tell the most effective stories

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:09.392
or just a cinephile looking to better
understand what you're watching,

00:05:09.392 --> 00:05:13.229
I think understanding perspective
and how it's used is a very important part

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:14.647
of understanding film.

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:17.066
Let's start by talking about

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:20.236
this distinction between objective
and subjective perspective.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:20.862
Most likely,

00:05:20.862 --> 00:05:23.948
you kind of have an innate sense
of the difference between these two,

00:05:23.948 --> 00:05:25.992
even if you haven't
thought about it before.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:31.914
But how do we really tell if an aspect
of a movie is objective or subjective?

00:05:32.081 --> 00:05:35.126
Here's how I tend to approach
reading the perspective of a scene

00:05:35.126 --> 00:05:37.587
or a specific choice
that a filmmaker is making.

00:05:37.587 --> 00:05:38.713
If a filmmaking decision,

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:42.508
whether it's a camera move, an edit,
what we hear or what we see

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.429
is motivated by the experience of one
or more characters

00:05:46.637 --> 00:05:49.766
within the scene, then it's subjective.

00:05:50.308 --> 00:05:53.603
But if how the scene is shot,
what we see, how the scene is cut

00:05:53.978 --> 00:05:57.982
is more neutral
or is reflective of an experience

00:05:57.982 --> 00:05:59.067
that kind of stands

00:05:59.067 --> 00:06:02.487
outside the experience
of any of the characters within the scene,

00:06:02.987 --> 00:06:04.739
then it's more objective.

00:06:07.909 --> 00:06:08.826
These choices can

00:06:08.826 --> 00:06:11.954
have a huge impact
on how a film or scene feels.

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:13.748
For example,
I think a big reason why, for me,

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:17.418
Tom Cruise's stunts in Mission
Impossible Fallout go unbelievably hard.

00:06:17.627 --> 00:06:20.463
While the big stunt in Mission
Impossible Dead Reckoning part one

00:06:20.463 --> 00:06:24.258
kind of falls flat
is because of the more effective use

00:06:24.258 --> 00:06:27.637
of subjective perspective
for the stunts in Fallout.

00:06:27.637 --> 00:06:28.513
In the Fallout scenes,

00:06:28.513 --> 00:06:32.600
we're right there with Cruise
and we can see he's doing it all himself.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:34.602
Whereas here, not only do we feel

00:06:34.602 --> 00:06:38.106
much more removed from the action
because of the very objective perspective,

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:40.608
but despite the fact that we know
Cruise himself

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:44.237
did this jump because of his reputation
and because the film's marketing really

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:48.741
drove that point home, it could have
easily been a stunt double doing this.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:51.369
We can't even see that it is Tom Cruise.

00:06:51.369 --> 00:06:52.412
So you can go to all this work

00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:55.790
to plan an incredible stunt
and get Tom Cruise to do it.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:58.251
But if you shoot it
from the wrong perspective,

00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:01.003
you kind of undercut all that work.

00:07:01.003 --> 00:07:03.714
I think it's useful to think
of subjectivity and objectivity

00:07:03.714 --> 00:07:05.591
as a kind of spectrum.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:08.886
For example,
I described this scene in Oppenheimer

00:07:08.886 --> 00:07:12.890
as subjective relative
to this scene from Twin Peaks.

00:07:13.182 --> 00:07:16.602
But that doesn't mean that this scene
couldn't be even more subjective.

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:19.939
If, for example,
Nolan had confined the experience

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:23.317
of the scene
to what Oppenheimer was experiencing

00:07:25.027 --> 00:07:27.947
in another, I think, very effective scene
in Oppenheimer.

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:30.950
That's literally what Nolan does

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:33.119
in this scene.

00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:34.704
We're hearing and seeing things

00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:39.041
that only Oppenheimer is experiencing
in his imagination, literally

00:07:39.041 --> 00:07:43.713
being inside a character's imagination
or mind is about as subjective as it gets.

00:07:44.422 --> 00:07:47.008
Apologies
while I take a quick semantic sidebar here

00:07:47.008 --> 00:07:50.136
to avoid some confusion, it's important
to note that when I'm talking

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:54.307
about objective and subjective
in this essay, the objective perspective

00:07:54.557 --> 00:07:59.437
is not necessarily the more accurate
or more factual perspective.

00:07:59.437 --> 00:08:03.774
If we're talking about events based on
true stories, scenes shot objectively

00:08:03.774 --> 00:08:09.238
can distort facts and get them wrong
just as much as scenes shot subjectively.

00:08:09.238 --> 00:08:12.492
And if we're talking about fictional
stories, scenes can be more objective

00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:16.037
or subjective,
even though the whole thing is a fiction.

00:08:16.037 --> 00:08:16.662
We'll explore this

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:20.500
a little bit more later because there's
some interesting implications here.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:24.921
But what you need to know for now
is that subjectivity and objectivity

00:08:24.921 --> 00:08:28.049
just refer
to how we perceive the events in the film,

00:08:28.257 --> 00:08:31.928
not whether or not
those events are actually factual.

00:08:33.596 --> 00:08:36.557
David Fincher's latest
film, The Killer, is a great example

00:08:36.557 --> 00:08:40.686
of a tightly subjective, visual
and sonic perspective.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.273
A lot of times
film sound is pretty objective.

00:08:44.482 --> 00:08:48.361
What you're hearing is just a vague
approximation of what the camera

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:51.948
would be hearing or more accurately,
what a microphone

00:08:52.156 --> 00:08:55.034
floating above the heads of the characters
would be hearing.

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:59.622
But it is possible for the audience
to hear something that sounds more like

00:08:59.622 --> 00:09:03.125
what one of the characters within
the scene is literally hearing.

00:09:08.256 --> 00:09:09.715
When we hear the music that's playing

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:13.886
just through his earbuds,
we're inside his sonic perspective.

00:09:14.095 --> 00:09:15.805
But that's not why I bring up this film.

00:09:15.805 --> 00:09:19.141
I bring it up because it's a great example
of another important kind

00:09:19.141 --> 00:09:24.522
of perspective, narrative perspective,
the narrative framing of an entire film

00:09:24.522 --> 00:09:30.152
can also land somewhere on this spectrum
between subjective and objective.

00:09:30.194 --> 00:09:34.156
A movie like The Killer
is extremely subjective because for one,

00:09:34.156 --> 00:09:38.869
we're placed inside his mental headspace
through the use of voiceover.

00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:42.123
That essentially
sounds like his internal monologue.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.710
It's the idle hours that most often lead
a man to ruin.

00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:50.298
But more importantly, the plot information
that we receive as the audience

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.883
is entirely filtered through

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:56.262
the information that the killer receives
throughout the film.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:57.513
Fincher never cuts away

00:09:57.513 --> 00:10:00.683
from the character of the killer
to fill us in on a plot point

00:10:00.683 --> 00:10:04.395
or give us a piece of information
that the killer himself doesn't have.

00:10:04.645 --> 00:10:08.107
Our perspective of the narrative
as a whole is tightly constrained

00:10:08.316 --> 00:10:11.319
to the killer's experience and knowledge.

00:10:11.485 --> 00:10:14.780
Almost the complete opposite of this
would be a movie like The Big Short.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.534
Mortgage backed
securities, subprime loans, tranches.

00:10:19.619 --> 00:10:21.078
It's pretty confusing, right?

00:10:21.078 --> 00:10:24.081
So here's Margot Robbie
in a bubble bath to explain.

00:10:24.624 --> 00:10:28.461
Basically here,
the delivery of information is completely

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:32.465
detached from whether or not
any individual character is receiving it.

00:10:32.506 --> 00:10:34.258
Instead,
the narrative perspective of the film

00:10:34.258 --> 00:10:37.762
is objective, almost to the point
of being more like an essay

00:10:37.762 --> 00:10:40.765
or a documentary than a narrative film.

00:10:41.015 --> 00:10:44.018
Another movie that's almost the opposite
from the killer thematic,

00:10:44.143 --> 00:10:48.272
but which also very effectively uses
a tight, subjective

00:10:48.272 --> 00:10:53.110
perspective narratively and visually,
is the quiet girl from last year.

00:10:53.361 --> 00:10:56.238
We experience almost everything
in the film narratively and visually

00:10:56.238 --> 00:10:59.283
from the perspective of the main character
who's a child.

00:10:59.575 --> 00:11:02.370
And this often comes down
to even just placing the camera

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.373
at the height of a child
when shooting adults in the film.

00:11:05.539 --> 00:11:09.293
Most movies narrative perspective falls
somewhere between the tight subjectivity

00:11:09.293 --> 00:11:13.214
of the killer and the broad overview,
like objectivity of the Big Short.

00:11:13.547 --> 00:11:18.886
A movie like No Country for Old Men has
a kind of split subjectivity, objectivity.

00:11:19.053 --> 00:11:22.056
Each individual scene
is very tightly subjective.

00:11:22.181 --> 00:11:22.848
In each moment,

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:26.602
we only follow one character and we only
experience what they're experiencing.

00:11:26.644 --> 00:11:31.399
For example, in this scene where Anton
Chigurh is hunting down Llewellyn.

00:11:31.607 --> 00:11:35.152
We stay with Llewellyn perspective
throughout the scene,

00:11:35.444 --> 00:11:37.154
Even though there have been other scenes

00:11:37.154 --> 00:11:41.242
in the movie where we've been following
Anton's subjective perspective.

00:11:41.409 --> 00:11:44.912
But even though the narrative approach
in each scene is very subjective

00:11:45.121 --> 00:11:48.833
because the film as a whole follows
a bunch of different subjective

00:11:48.833 --> 00:11:53.212
perspectives, the entire film has more of
an objective narrative perspective

00:11:53.337 --> 00:11:57.341
that's built by sampling all of these
very tightly subjective moments

00:11:57.591 --> 00:12:00.511
when I really want to drive home,
as we're talking about this, is that

00:12:00.511 --> 00:12:05.391
it's not about one perspective
or one use of perspective being best.

00:12:05.683 --> 00:12:10.396
It's really about understanding
which perspective is going to be best

00:12:10.396 --> 00:12:14.859
for the story that you're telling
and when to switch between perspectives

00:12:14.859 --> 00:12:17.862
if that's something that will be useful
for your story.

00:12:17.945 --> 00:12:22.241
Predictably, Hitchcock is going to come up
because he was a master of this

00:12:22.575 --> 00:12:24.285
in a story like Notorious.

00:12:24.285 --> 00:12:26.662
He tends to follow pretty tightly

00:12:26.662 --> 00:12:30.374
with the subjective perspective
of the two main characters.

00:12:30.666 --> 00:12:34.462
We only really get information
as they get information,

00:12:34.462 --> 00:12:37.423
and most of the scenes
include one of those characters.

00:12:37.715 --> 00:12:41.469
But in this scene, they're trying
to sneak away to a wine cellar at a party

00:12:41.719 --> 00:12:43.220
to gather some information,

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:47.224
and they know that the champagne
is going to run out at a certain point,

00:12:47.224 --> 00:12:48.267
which would lead to the servant

00:12:48.267 --> 00:12:51.479
getting more champagne
from the wine cellar and their discovery.

00:12:51.562 --> 00:12:56.025
Hitchcock could play this scene entirely
in their subjective perspective, but

00:12:56.025 --> 00:13:00.988
he chooses to cut away to a more objective
narrative perspective momentarily.

00:13:01.238 --> 00:13:05.159
Just a clue the audience
into what's happening with the champagne,

00:13:05.618 --> 00:13:10.039
just for the purpose
of creating more suspense, dramatic irony,

00:13:10.039 --> 00:13:11.332
or letting the audience

00:13:11.332 --> 00:13:15.377
in on a piece of information that one
or more of the characters doesn't know

00:13:15.586 --> 00:13:18.589
is essentially
a manipulation of perspective.

00:13:18.798 --> 00:13:21.801
But again, there's no one right path here
that's best.

00:13:21.884 --> 00:13:26.680
Sometimes the scene is more suspenseful
when we know that the character's enemy

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:30.768
is hiding behind the wall, and sometimes
the scene is more suspenseful.

00:13:30.976 --> 00:13:34.188
If we stay tightly within that character's
subjective experience

00:13:34.396 --> 00:13:38.317
and we're just as in the dark as they are,
it's something that filmmakers

00:13:38.317 --> 00:13:39.944
kind of have to fill out.

00:13:39.944 --> 00:13:41.237
But if you're not even thinking

00:13:41.237 --> 00:13:45.074
about the perspective of the scene,
you're going to miss this entirely.

00:13:46.659 --> 00:13:49.453
When it comes to narrative perspective,
whether or not the perspective

00:13:49.453 --> 00:13:51.580
is going to be more objective
or subjective

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:55.125
and when to switch between them is not
the only thing you need to think about.

00:13:55.543 --> 00:13:58.629
If you're using a subjective perspective,
which pretty much

00:13:58.629 --> 00:14:02.716
every film does to some degree,
One of the first considerations

00:14:02.716 --> 00:14:08.013
a writer needs to make is What perspective
am I going to tell this story from?

00:14:08.222 --> 00:14:08.848
Martin Scorsese.

00:14:08.848 --> 00:14:12.393
He was two years into working on the
screenplay for Killers of the Flower Moon

00:14:12.393 --> 00:14:15.855
when Leonardo DiCaprio came to him
and asked the question,

00:14:16.272 --> 00:14:17.982
What is the heart of this story?

00:14:17.982 --> 00:14:21.735
After some reflection course
as he realized that the heart of the story

00:14:21.735 --> 00:14:25.531
he was trying to tell really lay more
with the Osage tribe

00:14:25.531 --> 00:14:29.410
and less with the FBI agents
investigating the crimes,

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:32.413
which is what he had been focusing on
so far.

00:14:32.413 --> 00:14:33.289
Realizing this,

00:14:33.289 --> 00:14:38.210
they rewrote the script to include more
of the perspective of the Osage people,

00:14:38.335 --> 00:14:41.422
the perspective of the men investigating
the crime is a perspective

00:14:41.422 --> 00:14:45.342
we've seen time and time and time again
in movies and TV.

00:14:45.342 --> 00:14:46.218
So making this change

00:14:46.218 --> 00:14:50.180
didn't just make the story more original
and artistically compelling.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.725
I think it actually told
the more important story

00:14:52.725 --> 00:14:57.521
by focusing on the perspective
that recognizes the experience

00:14:57.521 --> 00:15:02.526
of those most directly affected
by these horrific serial killings.

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:05.613
And this brings us to a very interesting
consideration

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:08.032
when it comes to perspective in film.

00:15:08.032 --> 00:15:11.619
Even though the script was rewritten
to incorporate more of the Osage

00:15:11.619 --> 00:15:15.080
perspective,
technically the film still focuses

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.459
more on the villains
than the native perspective.

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:21.587
And even if it had focused entirely
on the native perspective,

00:15:22.004 --> 00:15:25.507
technically that Native perspective
would have been Martin Scorsese's

00:15:25.758 --> 00:15:29.219
depiction
of his perception of that perspective,

00:15:29.303 --> 00:15:31.096
which would be different
from that perspective

00:15:31.096 --> 00:15:35.059
being depicted by someone
who themselves is within that perspective.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:40.314
Even though a film can depict
more objective or subjective perspectives.

00:15:40.356 --> 00:15:42.316
Technically, both of those depictions

00:15:42.316 --> 00:15:45.736
are still from the perspective
of the filmmakers themselves.

00:15:45.903 --> 00:15:49.865
Scorsese He's trying to depict
this perspective accurately by consulting

00:15:49.865 --> 00:15:54.078
with and including people from the Osage
tribe in the filmmaking process.

00:15:54.078 --> 00:15:56.956
And I think it's great that he's
including this perspective

00:15:56.956 --> 00:15:58.207
rather than excluding it.

00:15:58.207 --> 00:16:00.292
I'm not saying it's a bad movie
because of this.

00:16:00.292 --> 00:16:02.419
It's one of my favorite movies
of the year.

00:16:02.419 --> 00:16:06.131
I think it's just important
and interesting to make this distinction

00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:09.468
between someone depicting
another person's perspective

00:16:09.718 --> 00:16:13.514
and someone
representing their own perspective.

00:16:13.639 --> 00:16:17.518
If you're interested in learning more
about this, particularly as it relates

00:16:17.518 --> 00:16:21.855
to native perspectives or the killers
of the Flower Moon, I highly recommend

00:16:21.855 --> 00:16:25.901
Elias Gould's video about this
on his channel Native Media Theory.

00:16:26.026 --> 00:16:29.697
This is a big part of why
representation is itself important,

00:16:29.697 --> 00:16:31.907
both in front of and behind the lens.

00:16:31.907 --> 00:16:35.327
The male gaze is a product of men
historically

00:16:35.327 --> 00:16:38.747
just representing
their perspective of women in cinema.

00:16:38.747 --> 00:16:42.167
White savior narratives and film
are also a product of perspective.

00:16:42.209 --> 00:16:45.212
Being able to see perspectives
that we can relate to,

00:16:45.379 --> 00:16:48.424
as well as perspectives
that are very different from our own

00:16:48.549 --> 00:16:52.011
is a huge part
of the power of cinema and art as a whole.

00:16:54.221 --> 00:16:56.015
I don't see the world the way

00:16:56.015 --> 00:17:00.728
most people do due to the ocular effects
of migraines that I often have.

00:17:00.853 --> 00:17:03.981
Sometimes when I look at the sky,
I see what

00:17:03.981 --> 00:17:08.152
I can only really describe
as a film grain effect.

00:17:08.152 --> 00:17:11.780
And when this isn't happening
due to my mild colorblindness, where

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:16.243
most people see something like this, I
see something a little bit more like this.

00:17:16.618 --> 00:17:19.371
These two things are a kind of constant
reminder

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:23.167
of the subjectivity
of my perception of the world.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:26.962
But even if you don't have these kind of
biological phenomena that I do, altering

00:17:26.962 --> 00:17:31.675
how you see the world, your perspective
is still subjective and unique to you.

00:17:31.717 --> 00:17:33.260
You can put a hundred people in a room

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:36.722
and they all have literally
a different perspective of that room.

00:17:36.764 --> 00:17:38.390
Even if they could stand
in the same place,

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:40.392
it would have to be at different times.

00:17:40.392 --> 00:17:44.646
And even if they saw exactly
the same thing, they still have different

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:49.359
internal emotional spaces,
memories and experiences in the past

00:17:49.359 --> 00:17:53.030
that might affect how they experience
what's happening in that room,

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:57.159
whether it's a piece of art, conversation
or interactions with other people.

00:17:57.201 --> 00:17:59.703
But this subjectivity
is sort of paradoxical

00:17:59.703 --> 00:18:04.625
because while those people
can have 100 unique perspectives,

00:18:04.625 --> 00:18:07.628
there's still a lot of experience
that is universal.

00:18:07.878 --> 00:18:11.548
There are many things
we all feel and experience in common.

00:18:11.799 --> 00:18:12.508
One of the things

00:18:12.508 --> 00:18:16.261
I really love about cinema,
something that keeps me interested in it

00:18:16.261 --> 00:18:20.474
beyond just being something to pass
the time, is the unique power

00:18:20.599 --> 00:18:23.685
it has to bring us
into another perspective.

00:18:23.685 --> 00:18:25.395
Sometimes this is interesting because it's

00:18:25.395 --> 00:18:29.024
bringing us into somebody else's
subjective perspective, and sometimes

00:18:29.024 --> 00:18:32.653
it's interesting because it's allowing us
a more objective perspective

00:18:32.903 --> 00:18:36.281
than any person could have in their own
experience.

00:18:36.406 --> 00:18:38.325
Perspective
is an ever present part of life.

00:18:38.325 --> 00:18:40.577
It shapes what we see and how we see it.

00:18:40.577 --> 00:18:44.039
So tools that allow us to shift
into a more objective perspective

00:18:44.289 --> 00:18:49.670
or allow us to glimpse another subjective
perspective aren't incredibly valuable.

00:18:49.962 --> 00:18:53.173
Seeing different perspectives
expands our understanding of the world.

00:18:53.173 --> 00:18:57.469
Comparing as many subjective perspectives
as possible is how we get to

00:18:57.469 --> 00:19:01.557
an understanding of what objective reality
even is in the first place.

00:19:01.598 --> 00:19:05.144
I love when a film gets to show me
an experience that I've never had, one

00:19:05.144 --> 00:19:09.606
that's new to me, but it's equally
valuable to me to find myself relating to

00:19:09.731 --> 00:19:12.943
and finding commonality
with the perspective of someone

00:19:12.943 --> 00:19:15.946
in a different time, place or culture.

00:19:15.988 --> 00:19:21.618
Every shot, film or scene automatically
has some kind of perspective.

00:19:21.618 --> 00:19:25.122
Perspective is just naturally
part of the process of telling a story

00:19:25.372 --> 00:19:29.710
in the same way light is just naturally
part of the process of cinematography.

00:19:29.751 --> 00:19:33.797
But you could just allow the light
to arbitrarily be whatever

00:19:33.797 --> 00:19:36.800
it naturally is, or you can use

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.637
light as a tool to help tell your story.

00:19:40.888 --> 00:19:42.973
The same is true of perspective.

00:19:42.973 --> 00:19:46.643
You can not think about it
and just shoot your film and it'll have

00:19:46.643 --> 00:19:48.270
whatever perspective it has.

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:51.273
But I think the greatest films understand
what their perspective is,

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.110
why they have that perspective,
and use it with narrative intention.

00:19:56.111 --> 00:19:58.113
But it doesn't end with cinema.

00:19:58.113 --> 00:19:59.198
While I've been talking about this,

00:19:59.198 --> 00:20:03.368
have you thought yet about the perspective
of this video?

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:06.622
Obviously, I'm
presenting my own perspective narratively,

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:09.791
but stylistically
it's pretty objective in delivery.

00:20:09.791 --> 00:20:14.421
But there's also a visual perspective
to the video that impacts the way it feels

00:20:14.838 --> 00:20:15.881
with the camera right here.

00:20:15.881 --> 00:20:21.136
As I'm talking to you, hopefully it feels
fairly direct, informal and comfortable.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:24.431
But if I were to put the camera out here
in the hallway,

00:20:24.431 --> 00:20:29.811
suddenly the more voyeuristic, removed
perspective changes how things feel.

00:20:29.811 --> 00:20:30.646
This is the technique

00:20:30.646 --> 00:20:33.732
that Benny Safdie and Nathan Fielder
are using throughout their new show,

00:20:33.732 --> 00:20:37.236
The Curse, where many scenes are shot
with this kind of removed

00:20:37.236 --> 00:20:40.656
voyeuristic camera peeking around corners
and through doorways.

00:20:40.864 --> 00:20:43.825
The two main characters in the show
are desperate to present

00:20:43.825 --> 00:20:46.662
a certain image of themselves
to the outside world,

00:20:46.662 --> 00:20:50.082
and they do this by performing
any time they're in front of a camera

00:20:50.332 --> 00:20:53.001
or someone
they want to make an impression on.

00:20:53.001 --> 00:20:57.005
The show highlights the absurdity of this
couple's attempt to perform their lives

00:20:57.005 --> 00:21:00.008
to others by showing us moments
where they're performing

00:21:00.008 --> 00:21:03.762
and moments where they're being themselves
in long, unbroken scenes.

00:21:03.971 --> 00:21:07.766
The removed voyeuristic perspective of the
camera is a very important part of this

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.894
because it makes us feel
like we're seeing them

00:21:10.894 --> 00:21:14.439
even when they don't think
they're being seen by anyone.

00:21:14.898 --> 00:21:18.485
The show is poking at an uncomfortable
reality that many of us know

00:21:18.485 --> 00:21:23.073
in the back of our minds that whenever
we watch something on reality TV or

00:21:23.073 --> 00:21:27.536
on social media, whether it's an Instagram
reel or a YouTube video like this,

00:21:27.828 --> 00:21:31.456
the perspective of the creator shapes
what you're watching.

00:21:31.665 --> 00:21:33.458
Well,
there's a beauty that I've talked about

00:21:33.458 --> 00:21:36.461
in how cinema can form and wield
perspectives.

00:21:36.461 --> 00:21:39.548
There are many people who are invested
in passing

00:21:39.548 --> 00:21:43.051
off their subjective perspective
as objective reality,

00:21:43.218 --> 00:21:47.180
and filmmaking gives people
the tools to do this shockingly well.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:51.143
That distinction I made at the beginning
where I said that an objective perspective

00:21:51.143 --> 00:21:54.146
does not necessarily mean more factual.

00:21:54.271 --> 00:21:56.440
This is something that trips us up a lot.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:59.318
This is the whole basis of reality TV.

00:21:59.318 --> 00:22:02.946
Reality TV often
distorts and fictionalizes reality

00:22:03.196 --> 00:22:06.241
just as much as fictional film can.

00:22:06.575 --> 00:22:10.037
But the whole genre is based on a style

00:22:10.037 --> 00:22:14.124
of filmmaking that makes it look
and feel like you're watching

00:22:14.124 --> 00:22:17.878
a more objective perspective,
like you're watching reality.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:21.423
This same dynamic is true of social media.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:24.676
It's good media literacy to realize that

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:29.389
every piece of media that you look
at has some kind of perspective.

00:22:29.389 --> 00:22:32.434
Even when something has the impression
of objectivity

00:22:32.434 --> 00:22:35.771
and doesn't feel like
it has a subjective perspective.

00:22:36.104 --> 00:22:40.692
It ultimately does, to a certain extent,
the perspective shaping tools

00:22:40.692 --> 00:22:42.903
that cinema has available come into play

00:22:42.903 --> 00:22:45.072
even the moment you turn
on your phone camera

00:22:45.072 --> 00:22:47.991
and we're all using them
without even being aware of it.

00:22:47.991 --> 00:22:51.370
The tightly controlled perspective
I'm showing you of my office right now

00:22:51.661 --> 00:22:56.375
makes me look like less of a disorganized
and messy person than I actually am.

00:22:56.416 --> 00:22:59.753
This kind of shaping of reality happens
every time we turn on a camera

00:22:59.753 --> 00:23:02.839
and choose to pointed at one thing
instead of something else.

00:23:04.383 --> 00:23:05.008
Perspective is

00:23:05.008 --> 00:23:09.054
ultimately an aspect of media
that we can't avoid, so we should be aware

00:23:09.054 --> 00:23:12.057
of it, acknowledge it,
and be mindful of how we use it.

00:23:15.477 --> 00:23:16.520
Jetlag is my favorite thing

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:21.066
to watch right now on Nebula,
a sort of reality travel game show.

00:23:21.066 --> 00:23:22.609
And one of the things that I like about

00:23:22.609 --> 00:23:26.738
it is unlike the reality TV shows
you watch on television.

00:23:27.072 --> 00:23:30.158
In this,
the participants themselves film the show.

00:23:30.283 --> 00:23:33.620
There's something so fun and immediate
about the tightly subjective perspective

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:37.040
of how it's shot that makes it feel like
you're really hanging out with Sam, Ben

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:41.253
and Adam and their guests as they chase
each other all around the world.

00:23:41.378 --> 00:23:43.422
There's a quality to it
that I don't think it would have

00:23:43.422 --> 00:23:45.549
if it was shot
from a different perspective.

00:23:45.549 --> 00:23:46.299
It's a lot of fun,

00:23:46.299 --> 00:23:51.221
and I watch each episode
when it comes out a week early on Nebula.

